{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792366520,"nonce":"HbGGGpSLRAZR4boxvrwte/Ej+/E=","message":"Layer content now goes into a pooled texture covering only the layer's bounds (b32764fc, local), plus where a drop shadow is read from (the region shifted back by the shadow's offset). The content is moved into that frame (boxes, clips, gradients, image destinations, nested layers; a glyph's atlas rect stays), the renderer's viewport is set to the texture's size for the draw, and the composite maps target pixels into it. Textures are rounded up to 64 px and pooled by exact size, and cleared explicitly so reuse cannot leak content. Checked by pixels: a blur moved off the origin, a gradient and text inside a faded layer, and the shadow, all against CPU oracles within 1 to 2 levels; the blur at sigma 12, 16 and 32 stays within 5. One semantic to know: with a texture that has no transparent margin the edge texels repeat past it, so the producer's bounds, which always hold three deviations of margin, are part of the contract. Measured on this Mac, a whole frame through the executor with one layer of an element, ms per frame, layer bounds the whole 1920x1080 target vs 400x300: fade 0.49-0.72 vs 0.44-0.46; blur 4: 1.49-1.50 vs 0.47-0.49; blur 16: 1.72 vs 0.51-0.53. So an element-sized layer costs about the same whatever its effect. Remaining: mask, backdrop, multi-stop gradients, canvases, local clip, clip-path frame, fade rect, notch.","files":null}]}